# Expectation record for 13_floating_point/002_float_int_conversions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_float_int_conversions
area               = 13_floating_point
description        = Floating-to-integer and integer-to-floating conversions strictly inside the representable ranges, with truncation toward zero, in a folded and a volatile-runtime variant, plus four round trips
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. The conversion rules ARE the subject of this program,
so the argument below is made site by site against a stated range table rather than in general,
and a reader can check every claim in it without opening the source a second time.

THE ONE RULE THIS PROGRAM LIVES OR DIES BY. C11 6.3.1.4p1: when a finite value of real floating
type is converted to an integer type the fractional part is discarded -- the value is truncated
TOWARD ZERO -- and the behaviour is undefined only if THE VALUE OF THE INTEGRAL PART, the value
that survives that truncation, cannot be represented in the destination. Nothing is reduced
modulo anything. Undefined behaviour here would not merely weaken the differential oracle, it
would destroy it: with undefined behaviour present both compilers are permitted to do anything,
so a divergence between them would be evidence about neither. Every destination type used here
has the same width on all four supported targets, so ONE range table holds everywhere, and it is
the table every claim below is checked against:

    int                         -2147483648 .. 2147483647
    unsigned int                          0 .. 4294967295
    short                            -32768 .. 32767
    unsigned short                        0 .. 65535
    signed char                        -128 .. 127
    unsigned char                         0 .. 255
    long long          -9223372036854775808 .. 9223372036854775807
    unsigned long long                    0 .. 18446744073709551615

FLOATING -> INTEGER, EVERY SITE. Each row is one conversion the program performs twice, once
folded over constants and once through a volatile operand, so the constant folder and the backend
are held to the same answer. The margin column is the distance from the truncated value to the
NEARER bound of its destination, and the whole point of printing it is that it is never negative
and never crossed:

    source            format  destination         truncates to  margin
    1000000.5         double  int                      1000000  vast
    -1000000.5        double  int                     -1000000  vast
    0.75              double  int                            0  vast
    -0.75             double  int                            0  vast
    2147480000.0      double  int                   2147480000  3647 below the maximum
    -2147480000.0     double  int                  -2147480000  3648 above the minimum
    1234567890123.5   double  long long          1234567890123  vast
    4000000000000.75  double  unsigned long long 4000000000000  vast
    4000000000.0      double  unsigned int          4000000000  294967295 below the maximum
    32000.5           double  short                      32000  767 below the maximum
    65000.5           double  unsigned short             65000  535 below the maximum
    100.75            double  signed char                  100  27 below the maximum
    200.25            double  unsigned char                200  55 below the maximum
    16777216.0f       float   int                     16777216  vast
    3.75f             float   int                            3  vast
    1000000.0f        float   unsigned int             1000000  vast
    -32000.0f         float   short                     -32000  768 above the minimum
    250.75f           float   unsigned char                250  5 below the maximum
    -0.75             double  unsigned int                   0  at the minimum, and defined
    -0.5f             float   unsigned char                  0  at the minimum, and defined
    -0.25             double  unsigned long long             0  at the minimum, and defined

The four round trips add four more conversions in this direction on their inward leg, each as far
inside its destination as the rows above: 12345.0 as a double to int, 1024.0 as a float to int,
1234567890123.0 as a double to long long, and 4000000000.0 as a double to unsigned int, the last
being the same conversion the ninth row already records. THE NARROWEST NON-ZERO MARGIN ANYWHERE IN
THE PROGRAM IS THEREFORE 5, separating 250 from unsigned char's maximum, and the narrowest on a
signed destination is the 27 separating 100 from signed char's.

THE NEGATIVE-TO-UNSIGNED QUESTION, ANSWERED RATHER THAN AVOIDED, because it is the one a reader
will want to check and the one most easily got wrong. THREE NEGATIVE SOURCES ARE CONVERTED TO
UNSIGNED DESTINATIONS, DELIBERATELY -- the last three rows above -- and every one of them is fully
defined rather than borderline. 6.3.1.4p1 turns on the representability of the INTEGRAL PART, and
a value strictly between -1 and 0 has integral part 0, which every unsigned type represents. The
footnote attached to that paragraph says so directly: it fixes the range of portable real floating
values for an unsigned destination at (-1, destination maximum + 1), and records that the modular
remaindering of the INTEGER-to-unsigned rule (6.3.1.3p2) need not be performed for a floating
source. So -0.75, -0.5f and -0.25 must each convert to 0, and the case earns its place precisely
because it is where an implementation is likeliest to substitute something else for truncation
toward zero: a saturating conversion would print the destination maximum, the modular integer rule
reached for by mistake would print it too, and rounding away from zero would print 1.
THE DANGEROUS REGION IS "INTEGRAL PART OUT OF RANGE", NOT "SOURCE NEGATIVE", and that region is
ABSENT FROM THIS PROGRAM ON BOTH SIDES. No negative source of magnitude one or more reaches any
unsigned destination: such a source has a negative integral part, which lies outside every
unsigned range and would be undefined. And no source of either sign has an integral part beyond
its destination's bounds, which is undefined for a signed and an unsigned destination alike. Every
other source feeding unsigned int, unsigned short, unsigned char or unsigned long long above is
non-negative.

NO NON-FINITE VALUE EXISTS ANYWHERE IN THE PROGRAM, which matters because converting a
not-a-number or an infinity to an integer type is undefined outright, no integral part of either
being representable in anything. No not-a-number, no infinity, no negative zero and no subnormal
is produced, converted or assumed; there is no division at all, floating or integer, so none can
arise that way either; and every floating value in the program is written as a terminating decimal
literal in the source.

INTEGER -> FLOATING is the safer direction and still carries one hazard of its own: an integer not
exactly representable in the destination format is converted with IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED rounding
(C11 6.3.1.4p2), which permits either adjacent value, so two conforming targets could legitimately
disagree. Every integer converted here is therefore EXACTLY representable in the format it is
converted to, and NO ROUNDING DECISION IS EVER TAKEN. binary32 carries 24 significand bits and
binary64 carries 53, and what has to fit is the width of the value's ODD PART, because trailing
factors of two are absorbed by the exponent and cost no significand at all:

    12345, 1024, 250, -125       well under 24 bits   exact in binary32 and binary64
    16777216 = 2^24              1 bit                exact in both
    4000000000 = 2^11 * 5^9      21 bits, from 5^9    exact in both
    9007199254740992 = 2^53      1 bit                exact in binary64
    4000000000000 = 2^14 * 5^12  28 bits, from 5^12   exact in binary64 only
    1234567890123, odd           41 bits              exact in binary64 only

The last two exceed binary32's 24 bits, and precisely for that reason neither is ever converted to
float -- each goes only to double.

NO ROUNDING OCCURS AT ANY STEP, IN EITHER DIRECTION, and that single fact is what defeats the one
target difference that could otherwise reach this program. i686 evaluates floating expressions in
x87 extended precision -- FLT_EVAL_METHOD 2, measured -- while the other three targets do not, so
an inexact intermediate could legitimately differ there. Every floating value here is a dyadic
rational, an integer or an integer plus 1/4, 1/2 or 3/4, exactly representable in binary32 and
binary64 alike, and excess precision cannot perturb a value that is already exact in every format
involved. The per-target measurement behind this is recorded in impl_defined_notes below.

EVERY CONVERSION SITE CARRIES AN EXPLICIT CAST, which is what keeps this program inside the active
audit gate rather than exempt from it. Area 13 sanctions no deviation, so this record declares
no `ub_audit_flags` key at all and the full default gate applies:
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror. An implicit narrowing or
an implicit sign change would be diagnosed and, under -Werror, fatal, so a conversion left
implicit would fail the gate rather than being tested. Every value of a type narrower than int is
widened with an explicit (int) before it is printed and every float with an explicit (double), so
each default argument promotion at the variadic call is stated in the source rather than performed
silently, and no length modifier is needed.

THE REMAINING RULEBOOK, each item argued for what this program actually does rather than for a
stricter shape it does not have. No signed integer overflow: no arithmetic is performed on any
integer at all, only conversion and equality comparison. No shift operator appears, so no shift
count can be out of range. No aliasing violation and no type punning: nothing is inspected
byte-wise, through a union, or through a pointer to another type, so nothing depends on a
representation or on padding bytes. No read of uninitialized storage: every object, the volatile
ones among them, is initialized at its declaration. No object is modified twice between sequence
points, nothing being assigned after its initializer. NOTHING DEPENDS ON UNSPECIFIED EVALUATION
ORDER: each volatile object is read EXACTLY ONCE, in a statement of its own, into a plain local of
its own type before any conversion touches it, so no expression in this program pairs two volatile
reads and no argument list carries more than one side-effecting argument -- every printf argument
is a plain local holding an already-computed result. No address of any object is taken, so nothing
depends on the relative addresses of unrelated objects. Every comparison in the agreement summary
is between two values of the same type, so no usual arithmetic conversion arises there and no
comparison mixes signedness.

NO HEADER IS NAMED and no preprocessor directive appears. bcc ships no stdio.h -- its bundled set
is the nine required freestanding headers plus a bonus stdatomic.h, ten files in all
(docs/project-guide.md line 212) -- so naming it would fail against bcc while succeeding against
the reference compiler, a divergence caused by the test rather than by the compiler. float.h and
limits.h are bundled and are still not named: every boundary value in the tables above is written
out as a literal in the source, and the bundled set is exercised by
12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c, whose subject that is. The single libc prototype
this program needs, int printf(const char *, ...);, is hand-declared.

HERMETIC, as constraint C4 requires: every input is a literal in the source. main is declared
int main(void) and returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires; nothing is opened, no
socket is created, no environment variable and no program argument is read, and no storage is
obtained from the heap.

BOTH GATES ARE CLEAN, and neither claim is merely asserted. The default warning gate named above
produces zero diagnostics in the single native compile-only invocation the suite makes of it, which
is why no deviation is declared. The sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address
-fno-sanitize-recover=all compiles, links and RUNS to completion with empty stderr and exit status 0
in its one instrumented native build; its float-cast-overflow check is precisely the check a breach
of the range table above would trip, and it reports nothing. That check runs only on the native
execution, so for the three cross targets it is the written argument above -- not the sanitizer --
that carries the claim.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
FLOATING-POINT DETERMINISM rests on three deliberate choices, each verified by measurement rather
than assumed:
1. Every floating value in the program is a DYADIC RATIONAL -- an integer, or an integer plus 1/4,
   1/2 or 3/4 -- so it is exactly representable in binary32 and binary64 alike and no rounding
   decision is ever taken, in either direction of conversion.
2. Every printed floating value carries an explicit %.6f, so no default precision is relied on and
   no printed digit is at the mercy of a rounding mode. The largest value printed that way,
   9007199254740992.000000, is exactly 2^53 and needs no rounding to render.
3. Excess intermediate precision cannot perturb an exact value. This matters because i686
   evaluates floating expressions in x87 extended precision while the other three targets do not
   -- FLT_EVAL_METHOD was measured as 2 on i686 and 0 on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64 -- and the
   exactness established in point 1 is what makes the difference unobservable here.
No not-a-number, no infinity, no negative zero, no subnormal and no division by zero is produced
or assumed anywhere.

TRUNCATION IS TOWARD ZERO, not toward negative infinity, and three groups of fields pin that down:
-1000000.5 gives -1000000 rather than -1000001, -0.75 gives 0 rather than -1, and the three
negative-to-unsigned sites give 0 rather than a destination maximum or a 1. A backend that rounded
instead of truncating, or that truncated the wrong way, changes exactly those fields and nothing
else, which is what localizes the defect to the conversion rather than to the program.

THE TWO-VARIANT RULE. Every conversion is performed twice, once over constants that the folder
evaluates at translation time and once through volatile-qualified operands that must be re-read
from memory at run time and so cannot be folded away. Without the runtime half the backend would
never be asked to convert anything and a conversion defect would escape entirely, the suite
reporting a pass on the strength of the folder's answer alone. The mirrored fold_X and rt_X fields
are printed on mirrored lines and the final folded_matches_runtime line reports one flag per
mirrored pair of lines, so a divergence surfaces on its own field first and in that summary
second.

TYPE WIDTHS ARE NORMALIZED, and that is what lets ONE golden record hold on all four targets.
sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) were measured as 4 on i686 and 8 on the other three
(docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457-462), so a destination whose range differed per target
could not carry a single expectation: long, unsigned long, size_t, ptrdiff_t and intptr_t are
therefore never named. The eight integer destinations that do appear -- int, unsigned int, short,
unsigned short, signed char, unsigned char, long long and unsigned long long -- are each the same
width on all four targets, which is exactly what makes the single range table in ub_notes sound.
Only %d, %u, %lld, %llu and %.6f are used: no %ld, %lu, %zu, %td or %p appears, and no address or
pointer value is printed.

NO PLAIN-CHAR VALUE IS COMPUTED OR PRINTED. Plain char signedness is implementation-defined and
was measured as signed on x86_64 and i686 and unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64, so the two character
conversions here name signed char and unsigned char explicitly and that measured difference cannot
reach this program. The only plain-char objects are the format-string literals, copied through
printf unchanged; no plain-char value is read as a number or compared.

LONG DOUBLE IS ABSENT, its representation having been measured at 16, 12, 16 and 16 bytes across
the four targets, with the two x86 targets carrying the x87 80-bit extended format inside that
storage and the other two carrying IEEE binary128. It belongs to
004_long_double_target_restricted in this area, which is the only record in the corpus that
disables an oracle: that record disables oracle (b) alone, keeps oracles (a) and (c) enabled on
all four targets at all three optimization levels, and carries the marker XD-TYPE-LONGDOUBLE-001
with the measured representation difference as its recorded reason.

NOTHING IS NARROWED HERE. No target is dropped, no oracle is disabled, no warning-gate deviation
is declared, and this record carries no expected-divergence marker, so a divergence in any cell of
this program is a genuine finding. Stdout is byte-identical and the exit status is 0 in all twelve
target-and-optimization-level cells, which is what lets oracle (b) compare the four backends with no
narrowing and oracle (c) hold these bytes as the golden record.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
d2i_pos=1000000 d2i_neg=-1000000 d2i_trunc_pos=0 d2i_trunc_neg=0
d2i_bound_pos=2147480000 d2i_bound_neg=-2147480000 d2ll=1234567890123 d2ull=4000000000000
d2u=4000000000 d2short=32000 d2ushort=65000 d2schar=100 d2uchar=200
negfrac2u=0 negfrac2uchar=0 negfrac2ull=0
f2i=16777216 f2i_trunc=3 f2u=1000000 f2short=-32000 f2uchar=250
i2f=12345.000000 i2d=-12345.000000 u2d=4000000000.000000 ll2d=9007199254740992.000000
i2f_pow2=16777216.000000 sc2d=-125.000000 uc2f=250.000000 ull2d=4000000000000.000000
roundtrip_i_d_i=12345 roundtrip_i_f_i=1024 roundtrip_ll_d_ll=1234567890123 roundtrip_u_d_u=4000000000
rt_d2i_pos=1000000 rt_d2i_neg=-1000000 rt_d2i_trunc_pos=0 rt_d2i_trunc_neg=0
rt_d2i_bound_pos=2147480000 rt_d2i_bound_neg=-2147480000 rt_d2ll=1234567890123 rt_d2ull=4000000000000
rt_d2u=4000000000 rt_d2short=32000 rt_d2ushort=65000 rt_d2schar=100 rt_d2uchar=200
rt_negfrac2u=0 rt_negfrac2uchar=0 rt_negfrac2ull=0
rt_f2i=16777216 rt_f2i_trunc=3 rt_f2u=1000000 rt_f2short=-32000 rt_f2uchar=250
rt_i2f=12345.000000 rt_i2d=-12345.000000 rt_u2d=4000000000.000000 rt_ll2d=9007199254740992.000000
rt_i2f_pow2=16777216.000000 rt_sc2d=-125.000000 rt_uc2f=250.000000 rt_ull2d=4000000000000.000000
rt_roundtrip_i_d_i=12345 rt_roundtrip_i_f_i=1024 rt_roundtrip_ll_d_ll=1234567890123 rt_roundtrip_u_d_u=4000000000
folded_matches_runtime=1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1
END
